# Workflow surface v2

## Why

On 2026-09-23 we checked Bellwether against real orchestration use. The primitives held up: direct-socket watches, proof-of-life prompts, reload survival. The workflow layer on top of them was thin:

1. `herdr-orchestration` documented a footgun as prose: "A watch started in the same turn as the prompt fires within a minute on the previous task's leftover idle state." Pi runs tool calls in parallel by default (`pi-agent-core` `executeToolCalls`). Herdr `agent.wait` also returns at once when the agent already sits in a requested state.
2. The owner gets one wake per worker, although the skill calls owner tokens the scarce resource.
3. Nothing reads the `bellwether/herdr/v1` intercom namespace. `publishWake` and `publishWorkflowReceipt` have no caller. Its presence chatter caused the 528k-entry session bloat.
4. Bellwether wakes bypass pi-until's session-wide follow-up arbiter.
5. The Herdr sidebar has no line for what a session is waiting on. No reporter publishes the configured workspace tokens `$progress`, `$agents`, or `$needs`.

## Decisions

- Do not move `herdr_watch` onto pi-until. An event-driven wait over the socket is better than shell polling. pi-until owns wake scheduling and recurring supervision, not Herdr observation.
- Do not grow the intercom broadcast. The useful integration is identity: which intercom session lives in which Herdr pane.

## Slice 1: prompt gate (built)

On an assistant `message_end`, Bellwether records every `herdr_agent prompt` call in that message as a pending prompt. In parallel and sequential tool modes alike, this runs before any tool executes. An `agent_state` watch started while a prompt is pending enters a `gated` phase. It arms its Herdr wait only after every pending prompt settles. The watch fails with `prompt_unproven` when a prompt to the same target returned no proof of life. At `turn_end`, any pending prompt whose call never executed settles as unproven.

Matching uses the raw prompt target, plus the proven pane ID and agent name, so a prompt by name and a watch by pane ID still match.

## Slice 2: sidebar reporter (built)

Source `user:bellwether.v1`. Probe receipts from Herdr 0.9.1 on 2026-09-23:

- Token names are one merged map across sources. The last writer wins, and a clear from any source removes the token.
- An older `seq` is ignored silently. `ttl_ms` expiry works.
- The direct socket accepts `pane.report_metadata` and `workspace.report_metadata`, and each returns `{type:"ok"}`.

Behavior:

- Pane `$wait` on the caller's own pane lists active watches, e.g. `⏳ reviewer · 4m` or `⏳ 3 waits · reviewer +2 · 12m`. It is cleared explicitly when the last watch ends.
- Workspace `$agents` (for example `2 working · 1 blocked`) and `$needs` (blocked agent names; `null` clears the row) come from the session that currently owns watches. Leases are refreshed, never cleared explicitly, so one session cannot wipe another's view.
- `$progress` stays owned by `herdr-workflow`.
- The reporter makes no request while no watch is active. Startup stays side-effect free.

## Slice 3: batched wakes (built, 1.4.0)

`src/wake.ts` holds wakes while the agent runs (`agent_start` to `agent_end`) and sends them as one `bellwether-wakes` follow-up about 250 ms after it goes idle. Idle-time bursts merge too. A missed `agent_end` delays a held wake by at most five minutes. It never drops one. Shutdown flushes held wakes directly.

Why hold while busy instead of a fixed window: wakes pile up during long owner turns. A fixed window would still give one turn per worker.

## Slice 4: pi-until arbiter (built; pi-until PR #5)

pi-until accepts version 1 requests on `pi-until:follow-up` and calls `accept()` synchronously once it queues them. Bellwether offers every batch there first and falls back to `pi.sendMessage` when nothing accepts, so either package works alone. Routed wakes arrive with `details: { followUpId, receipt }`.

The orchestration skill now recommends an `until repeat` supervision tick as a safety net for lost wakes. That is guidance, not code.

## Slice 5: intercom as a directory (built, 1.4.0)

The broadcast is gone: capability, binding, watch lifecycle, wake hints, and workflow-receipt hints. Bellwether registers `bellwether/directory/v1` only to call `listSessions()`, and publishes nothing.

We did not need an upstream pi-intercom change. Herdr already records a Pi pane's session file as `agent_session.value`. The filename ends in the Pi session ID, and pi-intercom uses the same ID (`PI_INTERCOM_SESSION_ID == PI_SESSION_ID`, checked 2026-09-23). `herdr_layout overview` adds `piSessionId` per Pi agent. With intercom connected, it adds `intercom: { name, status }`, or `null` when the session is unreachable.

## Slice 6: reported watches stay quiet (1.5.0, 2026-09-25)

Joel: "i get a lot of these", meaning watch wakes the owner answers with "already verified … the workflow is closed." Across fleet session files for 36 hours, 124 of 217 watch wakes were no-ops (at most one tool call and a short reply). Before 67 of them, the target had already sent the owner an intercom message. Before 15, a harvest or workflow close had already run. Several were races with a cancel.

Fix: the liveness probe records the target's Pi session (`piSessionIdFromAgent`). An inbound `intercom_message` from that session marks the watch reported. A later idle/done match finishes as `matched` with `quiet: "reported"`: receipt and lifecycle only, no wake. Blocked, target-gone, timeout, and failure still wake the owner, because a report does not cover them. Cancel also withdraws a wake still held in the router (`WakeRouter.withdraw`, keyed by watch id).

Not covered yet: owners that learn the outcome by reading the pane or harvesting without an intercom report. Measure again after a week before adding harvest awareness.

## Fleet rollout (2026-09-23)

- pi-until PR #5 merged as `eff388f` by `shitratgit[bot]`.
- `pi update git:github.com/joelhooks/{pi-bellwether,pi-until}@main --approve` ran on Flagg, Panda, and Blaine. Every host is now at pi-bellwether `84327b6` and pi-until `eff388f`, on Pi 0.87.1. A fresh `PI_OFFLINE=1 pi --help` load logged no extension errors on any host.
- Herdr `$wait` sidebar row:
  - Flagg: applied through `fleet-graft install`, then `reload-config`.
  - Blaine: rendered from `origin/main`'s template with `machines/blaine` theme, a backup was written, then `reload-config` applied it. Blaine's dark-wizard checkout was 28 commits behind with uncommitted prompt edits and a staged `machines/Mac -> blaine` alias, so `fleet-graft install` there was unsafe.
  - Panda: not applied. No Herdr server runs there, and uncommitted prompt edits in its dark-wizard make `fleet-graft install` unsafe.
- Panda has no pi-intercom. Its overview reports `intercom: "unavailable"`, which is the intended fallback.
- Running Pi sessions keep the old code until `/reload`.

## Receipts

- pi-bellwether: typecheck, 120 vitest tests, smoke, pack check, and audit pass.
- pi-until: `npm run check` passes: typecheck, ultracite, oxfmt, and 87 tests.
